<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fulwar Skipwith</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1765">1765</a>–<a href="/years/1839">1839</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fulwar Skipwith was an American diplomat and planter who was elected governor of the short-lived Republic of West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> in 1810. Earlier he had served as US consul in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> during the negotiation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana-purchase">Louisiana Purchase</a>. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> annexed the region at the end of 1810, he reluctantly yielded and later served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> legislature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fulwar-skipwith.jpg" alt="Fulwar Skipwith" width="280" height="380" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fulwar_Skipwith.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Fulwar Skipwith was born on 21 February 1765 into an influential family of Dinwiddie County in the Colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>; his cousin Henry Skipwith was a brother-in-law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a>. He left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/college-of-william-and-mary">College of William &amp; Mary</a> at the age of 16 to enlist in the army during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a> and served at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-yorktown">Siege of Yorktown</a> in 1781. After independence he entered the tobacco trade.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1790 Skipwith became US consul in the French colony of Martinique, where he saw the turmoil of the Revolution and the aftermath of an abortive slave insurrection before leaving in 1793. In 1795 he was made consul-general in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> under the US minister to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-monroe">James Monroe</a>. He assisted Robert Livingston in negotiating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana-purchase">Louisiana Purchase</a> and took part in the transfer of its southern part. In 1802 he married Evelina Louisa Barlié Van den Clooster, a Flemish baroness.</p><p>Skipwith moved to Spanish West Florida in 1809 and, as a member of its first judiciary, took part in the rebellion of 1810 against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, becoming the first and only governor of the Republic of West Florida. On 27 October 1810 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-madison">James Madison</a> proclaimed the annexation of the territory, which he claimed as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana-purchase">Louisiana Purchase</a>. Skipwith's government at St. Francisville wanted to negotiate entry into the Union as a separate state, but William C. C. Claiborne, governor of the Territory of Orleans, arrived with troops in December and refused to recognise it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Skipwith later sat in the Louisiana State Senate and became its second president. In December 1814, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-1812">War of 1812</a>, he and Magloire Guichard sponsored a resolution offering amnesty to the privateers of Barataria, which led Jean Lafitte and his men to join the defence of the city at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a>. In 1827 he was among the founders of the Agricultural Society of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baton-rouge">Baton Rouge</a>. He died at his plantation, Montesano, near Baton Rouge on 7 January 1839.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fulwar Skipwith” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The republic adopted a constitution modelled on American practice, elected <b>Fulwar Skipwith</b> as governor and set its capital at St. Francisville.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-west-florida">Republic of West Florida</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Dinwiddie County, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baton-rouge">Baton Rouge</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.08,-77.63&amp;year=1813" aria-label="Dinwiddie County on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Fulwar Skipwith"><image href="/maps/globes/g270n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5837 4351h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5837 4351h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.08,-77.63&amp;year=1813">What was here in 1813? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
